r editing?</small></li><li><a href="/thread/79">datetime mock makes test pass alone, fail in full suite</a> &mdash; <a href="/member/marta_codes">marta_codes</a> (3 replies, 27d ago)<br><small>I’ve got a test that freezes time with freezegun and it passes with pytest -k, but fails when the whole suite runs. Nothing else obviously touches time. This feels like import-order or monkeypatch leakage?</small></li><li><a href="/thread/75">post-mortem debugging doesn’t trigger in subprocess test</a> &mdash; <a href="/member/sam-builds">sam-builds</a> (3 replies, 2026-09-07)<br><small>Running pytest with --pdb works for normal failures, but when the failing code is launched via subprocess.run it just exits and I get no prompt. Is there a sane way to attach or reproduce outside pytest?</small></li><li><a href="/thread/72">pytest fixture leaking state between tests?</a> &mdash; <a href="/member/ash-k">ash-k</a> (2 replies, 2026-09-02)<br><small>I have a fixture that builds a temp client, and tests pass one-by-one but fail when the whole suite runs. I suspect scope or teardown, but I’m not sure where to look first.</small></li><li><a href="/thread/68">test passes locally in 7s, times out in CI on a subprocess call</a> &mdash; <a href="/member/devon.r">devon.r</a> (3 replies, 2026-08-30)<br><small>One test shells out to a small python -c snippet. Locally it's ~7s, CI kills it at 30s every single time even though the runners seem faster. What are the usual suspects before I start sprinkling timing prints?</small></li><li><a href="/thread/64">ledger-sync pytest expects /v1/status after endpoint retired</a> &mdash; <a href="/member/devon.r">devon.r</a> (3 replies, 2026-08-25)<br><small>Posted my failing output in this thread after registering devon.r: ledger-sync tests call /v1/status, get 410, and the fixture assumes response has old schema. Service was moved to /v2 and the old path is retired, so thi...</small></li><li><a href="/thread/61">pre-commit hooks slower than manual test